{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01An4oFiN4n9YNHN4L1eZsVu","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: prd001-testutils\n     2→title: \"pkg/testutils: Differential Testing Harness\"\n     3→\n     4→problem: |\n     5→  Every utility in go-unix-utils is verified by running the Go binary and the Homebrew\n     6→  GNU reference binary side by side and comparing their stdout, stderr, and exit code.\n     7→  Without a shared harness, each cmd/ package would duplicate the execution and comparison\n     8→  logic, the normalization hooks needed for non-deterministic output (timestamps in ts,\n     9→  path ordering in find), and the divergence reporting format. This duplication makes\n    10→  the verification layer fragile: a bug in one cmd/'s harness copy would produce\n    11→  different verification behavior than another cmd/'s copy.\n    12→\n    13→  pkg/testutils is the acceptance gate for the entire pipeline. The cobbler orchestrator\n    14→  cannot declare a utility complete until its differential tests pass through this harness.\n    15→  This PRD is also the authoritative API reference for stitch agents implementing cmd/\n    16→  packages: the agent reads this document, knows the full pkg/testutils API, and imports\n    17→  the package without reinventing it.\n    18→\n    19→  A cmd/ test file imports pkg/testutils and defines test cases as data. The stitch agent\n    20→  producing a new cmd/ utility writes only the test file — it does not recreate the package.\n    21→  The canonical usage pattern is:\n    22→\n    23→    package main_test\n    24→\n    25→    import (\n    26→        \"os\"\n    27→        \"testing\"\n    28→        \"github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/testutils\"\n    29→    )\n    30→\n    31→    func TestDiff(t *testing.T) {\n    32→        goBin := os.Getenv(\"GO_BINARY\")\n    33→        refBin := os.Getenv(\"REF_BINARY\")\n    34→        tests := []testutils.DiffTest{\n    35→            {Name: \"basic\", Args: []string{\"-n\", \"testdata/input.txt\"}, ExitCode: 0},\n    36→            {Name: \"stdin\", Args: []string{\"-c\"}, Stdin: []byte(\"hello\\n\"), ExitCode: 0},\n    37→            {Name: \"normalized\", Args: []string{\"-s\", \"%H:%M:%S\"}, Stdin: []byte(\"event\\n\"),\n    38→                ExitCode: 0, Normalize: []testutils.NormalizeFunc{testutils.TimestampNormalizer}},\n    39→        }\n    40→        testutils.RunDiffTests(t, goBin, refBin, tests)\n    41→    }\n    42→\n    43→goals:\n    44→  - G1: Provide a single execution function that runs a Go binary and a GNU reference binary with identical inputs and returns both outputs for comparison.\n    45→  - G2: Provide a comparison function that diffs stdout, stderr, and exit code and reports divergence with the triggering input.\n    46→  - G3: Provide a normalization hook mechanism so individual utilities can strip or replace non-deterministic output fields before comparison.\n    47→  - G4: Integrate with go test so that each cmd/ package can define test cases as data and call the harness from a standard TestXxx function.\n    48→\n    49→requirements:\n    50→  R1:\n    51→    title: Test case data structure\n    52→    items:\n    53→      - R1.1: |\n    54→          Must define DiffTest as the following named struct in package testutils:\n    55→\n    56→            type DiffTest struct {\n    57→                Name          string                 // subtest name used with t.Run; required\n    58→                Args          []string               // command-line arguments passed to both binaries\n    59→                Stdin         []byte                 // nil = no stdin (both binaries receive EOF immediately)\n    60→                Env           []string               // nil = use defaults only (LC_ALL=C); non-nil = KEY=VALUE pairs merged into inherited environment\n    61→                WorkDir       string                 // empty = per-test t.TempDir(); non-empty = use this directory for both binaries\n    62→                ExitCode      int                    // expected exit code for both binaries\n    63→                Normalize     []func([]byte) []byte  // applied in order to stdout and stderr of both binaries before comparison; nil or empty = no normalization\n    64→                ExpectedFiles map[string][]byte      // optional: path -> expected byte content after execution, for file-output utilities (sponge, cp)\n    65→            }\n    66→      - R1.2: DiffTest.Stdin nil means both binaries receive EOF immediately on stdin. An empty non-nil slice ([]byte{}) also produces no bytes but is semantically distinct (open stdin that is immediately closed).\n    67→      - R1.3: |\n    68→          DiffTest.Env nil means both binaries inherit the test process environment with\n    69→          default overrides applied (see R2.6). When non-nil, the KEY=VALUE pairs in the\n    70→          slice are merged into the inherited environment: matching keys are overridden, new\n    71→          keys are added. This merge behavior matches the struct comment (\"override or\n    72→          extend\") and avoids requiring callers to replicate the full environment for a\n    73→          single override.\n    74→      - R1.4: Must define type NormalizeFunc = func([]byte) []byte as a named type alias in package testutils. The Normalize field in DiffTest uses this element type. When the slice is nil or empty, no normalization is applied to stdout or stderr.\n    75→\n    76→  R2:\n    77→    title: Binary execution\n    78→    items:\n    79→      - R2.1: |\n    80→          Must provide func RunDiffTests(t *testing.T, goBinary, refBinary string, tests []DiffTest) that accepts the path to the Go binary under test (goBinary) and the GNU reference binary (refBinary), then runs each DiffTest as a named subtest via t.Run(test.Name, ...). Both binaries are invoked with identical Args, Stdin, and Env for each test case.\n    81→      - R2.2: Must capture stdout, stderr, and exit code from each binary independently.\n    82→      - R2.3: Must impose a configurable timeout on each binary invocation. The default timeout must be 10 seconds. The test fails if either binary exceeds the timeout.\n    83→      - R2.4: Must not write any output to the test process stdout or stderr during a passing test run.\n    84→      - R2.5: |\n    85→          Must support passing a working directory to each binary invocation via a WorkDir\n    86→          field on DiffTest. When WorkDir is empty, both binaries run in a per-test temporary\n    87→          directory created by the harness via t.TempDir(). The harness must use the same\n    88→          working directory for both binaries in a given test case. The DiffTest struct gains:\n    89→\n    90→            WorkDir string // empty = per-test t.TempDir(); non-empty = use this directory for both binaries\n    91→      - R2.6: |\n    92→          Must set LC_ALL=C in the environment for both binaries unless the DiffTest.Env\n    93→          explicitly overrides LC_ALL. This ensures deterministic sort order and formatting\n    94→          across locales. The harness applies this default before merging DiffTest.Env.\n    95→\n    96→  R3:\n    97→    title: Comparison and reporting\n    98→    items:\n    99→      - R3.1: Must apply the DiffTest.Normalize function (if non-nil) to both binaries' stdout and stderr before comparison.\n   100→      - R3.2: Must compare normalized stdout byte-for-byte. Any difference must be reported as a test failure.\n   101→      - R3.3: Must compare normalized stderr byte-for-byte. Any difference must be reported as a test failure.\n   102→      - R3.4: Must compare exit codes exactly. A difference must be reported as a test failure.\n   103→      - R3.5: 'Must report divergence with the following information in the failure message: the DiffTest args, the stdin content (truncated to 256 bytes if longer), the reference binary stdout, the Go binary stdout, the reference stderr, the Go stderr, and both exit codes.'\n   104→      - R3.6: Must run each DiffTest as a named subtest (t.Run) so individual test case failures are identifiable in go test output.\n   105→\n   106→  R4:\n   107→    title: Normalization hooks\n   108→    items:\n   109→      - R4.1: Must allow per-DiffTest normalization via the Normalize field. The function receives the raw output bytes and returns the normalized bytes.\n   110→      - R4.2: Must provide a built-in TimestampNormalizer that replaces common strftime timestamp patterns with a fixed placeholder string. This normalizer is used by cmd/ts tests.\n   111→      - R4.3: |\n   112→          Must allow normalizers to be composed: the harness must accept a slice of Normalize\n   113→          functions applied in order, not a single function. When the slice is nil or empty,\n   114→          no normalization is applied.\n   115→      - R4.4: |\n   116→          Must provide func ComposeNormalizers(fns ...NormalizeFunc) NormalizeFunc that returns\n   117→          a single NormalizeFunc applying the given functions in order. This is a convenience\n   118→          for cmd/ test files that combine multiple normalizers:\n   119→\n   120→            normalize := testutils.ComposeNormalizers(\n   121→                testutils.TimestampNormalizer,\n   122→                customNormalizer,\n   123→            )\n   124→\n   125→  R5:\n   126→    title: File-state comparison\n   127→    items:\n   128→      - R5.1: |\n   129→          Must support utilities whose primary output is a file rather than stdout (sponge,\n   130→          cp, mv). DiffTest.ExpectedFiles is a map[string][]byte where keys are file paths\n   131→          relative to the working directory and values are the expected byte content after\n   132→          execution. When ExpectedFiles is non-nil, the harness checks each entry after both\n   133→          binaries complete.\n   134→      - R5.2: |\n   135→          Must compare each ExpectedFiles entry byte-for-byte against the file produced by\n   136→          the Go binary. Any difference must be reported as a test failure with the same\n   137→          format as stdout divergence: file path, expected content (from reference), actual\n   138→          content (from Go binary). Missing files must also be reported as failures.\n   139→\n   140→non_goals:\n   141→  - pkg/testutils does not build or compile the Go binaries under test.\n   142→  - pkg/testutils does not install or manage Homebrew reference binaries.\n   143→  - pkg/testutils does not produce test reports beyond standard go test output.\n   144→  - pkg/testutils does not implement retry logic; a failing test fails immediately.\n   145→  - pkg/testutils does not mock or stub the filesystem or OS for unit testing cmd/ packages.\n   146→\n   147→acceptance_criteria:\n   148→  - \"AC1: A cmd/ package can define a []DiffTest slice and call RunDiffTests(t *testing.T, goBinary, refBinary string, tests []DiffTest) from a TestXxx function with no other setup. RunDiffTests runs each DiffTest as a named subtest (t.Run(test.Name, ...)) and calls t.Fatal on the first divergence in any subtest.\"\n   149→  - \"AC2: A passing test run produces no output. A failing test prints the triggering args, stdin, and both binaries' stdout, stderr, and exit code.\"\n   150→  - \"AC3: TimestampNormalizer replaces strftime-formatted timestamps (e.g., \\\"Feb 19 12:34:56\\\") with a fixed placeholder so that ts differential tests pass despite wall-clock differences.\"\n   151→  - \"AC4: A DiffTest with ExpectedFiles correctly fails when the Go binary writes different file content than expected.\"\n   152→  - \"AC5: A DiffTest that exceeds the timeout fails with a clear timeout message rather than hanging the test run.\"\n   153→  - \"AC6: All pkg/testutils code compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n   154→  - \"AC7: ComposeNormalizers combines multiple NormalizeFunc values into a single function that applies them in order.\"\n   155→  - \"AC8: RunDiffTests sets LC_ALL=C by default. A DiffTest with Env=[\\\"LC_ALL=en_US.UTF-8\\\"] overrides this default.\"\n   156→  - \"AC9: The usage example in the problem statement is sufficient for a stitch agent to write a cmd/ test file that imports pkg/testutils without recreating the package.\"\n   157→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9c61540f-c377-4834-9325-d677bf505084","uuid":"5e18e763-4926-4326-8244-ae5a18ab53a7","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/225/docs/specs/product-requirements/prd001-testutils.yaml","content":"id: prd001-testutils\ntitle: \"pkg/testutils: Differential Testing Harness\"\n\nproblem: |\n  Every utility in go-unix-utils is verified by running the Go binary and the Homebrew\n  GNU reference binary side by side and comparing their stdout, stderr, and exit code.\n  Without a shared harness, each cmd/ package would duplicate the execution and comparison\n  logic, the normalization hooks needed for non-deterministic output (timestamps in ts,\n  path ordering in find), and the divergence reporting format. This duplication makes\n  the verification layer fragile: a bug in one cmd/'s harness copy would produce\n  different verification behavior than another cmd/'s copy.\n\n  pkg/testutils is the acceptance gate for the entire pipeline. The cobbler orchestrator\n  cannot declare a utility complete until its differential tests pass through this harness.\n  This PRD is also the authoritative API reference for stitch agents implementing cmd/\n  packages: the agent reads this document, knows the full pkg/testutils API, and imports\n  the package without reinventing it.\n\n  A cmd/ test file imports pkg/testutils and defines test cases as data. The stitch agent\n  producing a new cmd/ utility writes only the test file — it does not recreate the package.\n  The canonical usage pattern is:\n\n    package main_test\n\n    import (\n        \"os\"\n        \"testing\"\n        \"github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils/pkg/testutils\"\n    )\n\n    func TestDiff(t *testing.T) {\n        goBin := os.Getenv(\"GO_BINARY\")\n        refBin := os.Getenv(\"REF_BINARY\")\n        tests := []testutils.DiffTest{\n            {Name: \"basic\", Args: []string{\"-n\", \"testdata/input.txt\"}, ExitCode: 0},\n            {Name: \"stdin\", Args: []string{\"-c\"}, Stdin: []byte(\"hello\\n\"), ExitCode: 0},\n            {Name: \"normalized\", Args: []string{\"-s\", \"%H:%M:%S\"}, Stdin: []byte(\"event\\n\"),\n                ExitCode: 0, Normalize: []testutils.NormalizeFunc{testutils.TimestampNormalizer}},\n        }\n        testutils.RunDiffTests(t, goBin, refBin, tests)\n    }\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Provide a single execution function that runs a Go binary and a GNU reference binary with identical inputs and returns both outputs for comparison.\n  - G2: Provide a comparison function that diffs stdout, stderr, and exit code and reports divergence with the triggering input.\n  - G3: Provide a normalization hook mechanism so individual utilities can strip or replace non-deterministic output fields before comparison.\n  - G4: Integrate with go test so that each cmd/ package can define test cases as data and call the harness from a standard TestXxx function.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Test case data structure\n    items:\n      - R1.1: |\n          Must define DiffTest as the following named struct in package testutils:\n\n            type DiffTest struct {\n                Name          string                 // subtest name used with t.Run; required\n                Args          []string               // command-line arguments passed to both binaries\n                Stdin         []byte                 // nil = no stdin (both binaries receive EOF immediately)\n                Env           []string               // nil = use defaults only (LC_ALL=C); non-nil = KEY=VALUE pairs merged into inherited environment\n                WorkDir       string                 // empty = per-test t.TempDir(); non-empty = use this directory for both binaries\n                ExitCode      int                    // expected exit code for both binaries\n                Normalize     []func([]byte) []byte  // applied in order to stdout and stderr of both binaries before comparison; nil or empty = no normalization\n                ExpectedFiles map[string][]byte      // optional: path -> expected byte content after execution, for file-output utilities (sponge, cp)\n            }\n      - R1.2: DiffTest.Stdin nil means both binaries receive EOF immediately on stdin. An empty non-nil slice ([]byte{}) also produces no bytes but is semantically distinct (open stdin that is immediately closed).\n      - R1.3: |\n          DiffTest.Env nil means both binaries inherit the test process environment with\n          default overrides applied (see R2.6). When non-nil, the KEY=VALUE pairs in the\n          slice are merged into the inherited environment: matching keys are overridden, new\n          keys are added. This merge behavior matches the struct comment (\"override or\n          extend\") and avoids requiring callers to replicate the full environment for a\n          single override.\n      - R1.4: Must define type NormalizeFunc = func([]byte) []byte as a named type alias in package testutils. The Normalize field in DiffTest uses this element type. When the slice is nil or empty, no normalization is applied to stdout or stderr.\n\n  R2:\n    title: Binary execution\n    items:\n      - R2.1: |\n          Must provide func RunDiffTests(t *testing.T, goBinary, refBinary string, tests []DiffTest) that accepts the path to the Go binary under test (goBinary) and the GNU reference binary (refBinary), then runs each DiffTest as a named subtest via t.Run(test.Name, ...). Both binaries are invoked with identical Args, Stdin, and Env for each test case.\n      - R2.2: Must capture stdout, stderr, and exit code from each binary independently.\n      - R2.3: Must impose a configurable timeout on each binary invocation. The default timeout must be 10 seconds. The test fails if either binary exceeds the timeout.\n      - R2.4: Must not write any output to the test process stdout or stderr during a passing test run.\n      - R2.5: |\n          Must support passing a working directory to each binary invocation via a WorkDir\n          field on DiffTest. When WorkDir is empty, both binaries run in a per-test temporary\n          directory created by the harness via t.TempDir(). The harness must use the same\n          working directory for both binaries in a given test case. The DiffTest struct gains:\n\n            WorkDir string // empty = per-test t.TempDir(); non-empty = use this directory for both binaries\n      - R2.6: |\n          Must set LC_ALL=C in the environment for both binaries unless the DiffTest.Env\n          explicitly overrides LC_ALL. This ensures deterministic sort order and formatting\n          across locales. The harness applies this default before merging DiffTest.Env.\n\n  R3:\n    title: Comparison and reporting\n    items:\n      - R3.1: Must apply the DiffTest.Normalize function (if non-nil) to both binaries' stdout and stderr before comparison.\n      - R3.2: Must compare normalized stdout byte-for-byte. Any difference must be reported as a test failure.\n      - R3.3: Must compare normalized stderr byte-for-byte. Any difference must be reported as a test failure.\n      - R3.4: Must compare exit codes exactly. A difference must be reported as a test failure.\n      - R3.5: 'Must report divergence with the following information in the failure message: the DiffTest args, the stdin content (truncated to 256 bytes if longer), the reference binary stdout, the Go binary stdout, the reference stderr, the Go stderr, and both exit codes.'\n      - R3.6: Must run each DiffTest as a named subtest (t.Run) so individual test case failures are identifiable in go test output.\n\n  R4:\n    title: Normalization hooks\n    items:\n      - R4.1: Must allow per-DiffTest normalization via the Normalize field. The function receives the raw output bytes and returns the normalized bytes.\n      - R4.2: Must provide a built-in TimestampNormalizer that replaces common strftime timestamp patterns with a fixed placeholder string. This normalizer is used by cmd/ts tests.\n      - R4.3: |\n          Must allow normalizers to be composed: the harness must accept a slice of Normalize\n          functions applied in order, not a single function. When the slice is nil or empty,\n          no normalization is applied.\n      - R4.4: |\n          Must provide func ComposeNormalizers(fns ...NormalizeFunc) NormalizeFunc that returns\n          a single NormalizeFunc applying the given functions in order. This is a convenience\n          for cmd/ test files that combine multiple normalizers:\n\n            normalize := testutils.ComposeNormalizers(\n                testutils.TimestampNormalizer,\n                customNormalizer,\n            )\n\n  R5:\n    title: File-state comparison\n    items:\n      - R5.1: |\n          Must support utilities whose primary output is a file rather than stdout (sponge,\n          cp, mv). DiffTest.ExpectedFiles is a map[string][]byte where keys are file paths\n          relative to the working directory and values are the expected byte content after\n          execution. When ExpectedFiles is non-nil, the harness checks each entry after both\n          binaries complete.\n      - R5.2: |\n          Must compare each ExpectedFiles entry byte-for-byte against the file produced by\n          the Go binary. Any difference must be reported as a test failure with the same\n          format as stdout divergence: file path, expected content (from reference), actual\n          content (from Go binary). Missing files must also be reported as failures.\n\nnon_goals:\n  - pkg/testutils does not build or compile the Go binaries under test.\n  - pkg/testutils does not install or manage Homebrew reference binaries.\n  - pkg/testutils does not produce test reports beyond standard go test output.\n  - pkg/testutils does not implement retry logic; a failing test fails immediately.\n  - pkg/testutils does not mock or stub the filesystem or OS for unit testing cmd/ packages.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - \"AC1: A cmd/ package can define a []DiffTest slice and call RunDiffTests(t *testing.T, goBinary, refBinary string, tests []DiffTest) from a TestXxx function with no other setup. RunDiffTests runs each DiffTest as a named subtest (t.Run(test.Name, ...)) and calls t.Fatal on the first divergence in any subtest.\"\n  - \"AC2: A passing test run produces no output. A failing test prints the triggering args, stdin, and both binaries' stdout, stderr, and exit code.\"\n  - \"AC3: TimestampNormalizer replaces strftime-formatted timestamps (e.g., \\\"Feb 19 12:34:56\\\") with a fixed placeholder so that ts differential tests pass despite wall-clock differences.\"\n  - \"AC4: A DiffTest with ExpectedFiles correctly fails when the Go binary writes different file content than expected.\"\n  - \"AC5: A DiffTest that exceeds the timeout fails with a clear timeout message rather than hanging the test run.\"\n  - \"AC6: All pkg/testutils code compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n  - \"AC7: ComposeNormalizers combines multiple NormalizeFunc values into a single function that applies them in order.\"\n  - \"AC8: RunDiffTests sets LC_ALL=C by default. A DiffTest with Env=[\\\"LC_ALL=en_US.UTF-8\\\"] overrides this default.\"\n  - \"AC9: The usage example in the problem statement is sufficient for a stitch agent to write a cmd/ test file that imports pkg/testutils without recreating the package.\"\n","numLines":157,"startLine":1,"totalLines":157}}}
