ng up a binResult type to hold the captured stdout, stderr, and exit code from each binary invocation, then starting to implement the main test runner function.\n\nWriting test runner logic... Comparing the outputs now—checking if exit codes match between the reference and Go implementations, then verifying stdout and stderr are identical after normalization, and if anything diverges I'll report the full context for debugging. Writing test assertions...\n\nNow I'm defining a helper function that executes a binary with arguments, stdin, environment variables, and a working directory, capturing its output and exit code with a timeout applied to each invocation.\n\nThen I'm running the command and checking if it exceeded the timeout, extracting the exit code from any error that occurred, and returning the captured stdout, stderr, and exit code as a result struct.\n\nNow I'm building the environment for binary execution by starting with the system environment, setting LC_ALL to C as a default, and then applying any test-specific environment variable overrides. The setEnvVar helper function handles updating or adding variables to the environment slice.\n\nI'm also implementing a normalizer system where individual normalization functions can be applied sequentially to transform data, and there's a convenience function to compose multiple normalizers into a single function that applies them in order.\n\nNow I'm setting up regex patterns to match common timestamp formats like the default syslog format, ISO-style timestamps, and others.\n\nI'm creating a normalizer function that iterates through these patterns and replaces any matching timestamps with a placeholder string, which is useful for comparing logs that might have different timestamps but identical content.\n\nI'm also defining a helper function to truncate stdin for display purposes, handling nil cases and length checks.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering the error handling in `runBinary` — according to the requirements, tim